ypass all enforcement. Two MIN remain open: the **Target:** regex uses `[ \\\\\\\\t]*$` (CRLF blind) while cascade-status uses `\\\\\\\\s*$` (CRLF-safe), breaking enforcement on Windows; adopter-slug collision between org/repo-foo and org/repo.foo is runtime-undetected. R11-SAFE-MIN-002 (v1.2.0 absent silent) is downgraded to nit — current code emits an explicit skip-warning at lines 155-158 when the tag is absent.\\\",\\\"findings\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"R1-SAFE-MAJ-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"`cascade-status: not applicable` + `--allow-not-applicable` + any valid `--tooling-slice-id` bypasses ALL cascade enforcement. The only machine check is DISPATCH-NOTE `slice-type: tooling` — a field the PR author controls. No slice-id field cross-checks `--tooling-slice-id` against DISPATCH-NOTE identity. A cohort-cascade author who also configures the CI workflow can set `slice-type: tooling` and pass the flag, exiting 0 with no log, target, or TF checks performed.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"check-invocation-log-entry.sh lines 205-215: `not applicable` case exits 0 after slice-type and tooling-slice-id checks. The DISPATCH-NOTE's `slice-type:` field (line 187-196, Python subprocess) is authored by the same party being verified. No DISPATCH-NOTE field encodes the slice's own ID (e.g., `slice-id: 024C`) that the script could compare against the `--tooling-slice-id` allowlist. R11-SAFE-MAJ-001 carry-forward; unmodified in fix-rounds 1-11.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"A cohort cascade slice can declare `not applicable` with a forged `slice-type: tooling`, receive CI green with zero enforcement of log presence, target match, or TF references. The cascade audit trail is absent for that adopter. Mitigated by D-031 single-operator + 3x R1 review, but the script's guard is beatable if CI wiring (024B-extras) passes `--allow-not-applicable` based on DISPATCH-NOTE content alone.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add `slice-id: <value>` as a machine-readable DISPATCH-NOTE field and verify it equals `--tooling-slice-id`. Alternatively, gate `--allow-not-applicable` in the 024B-extras CI workflow based on an independent source (branch name pattern) rather than DISPATCH-NOTE fields controlled by the PR author.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"R1-SAFE-MIN-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"The `**Target:**` extraction regex uses `[ \\\\\\\\t]*$` terminal anchor; `cascade-status` extraction uses `\\\\\\\\s*$`. On CRLF DISPATCH-NOTEs, `\\\\\\\\r` is not in `[ \\\\\\\\t]`, so `**Target:**` lines never match. All cascade-status paths that require target extraction (onboarded, onboarded-operator-bump, blocked-on-adopter-conflict) fail with opaque 'expected exactly one **Target:** match, found 0' on Windows or repos with CRLF line endings.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"check-invocation-log-entry.sh line 212 (Python): `r'^[ \\\\\\\\t]*-[ \\\\\\\\t]+\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*Target:\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*[ \\\\\\\\t]+([a-zA-Z0-9._/-]+)[ \\\\\\\\t]*$'` — `[ \\\\\\\\t]*$` terminal. Line 165: `r'^cascade-status:\\\\\\\\s+([^\\\\\\\\r\\\\\\\\n]+?)\\\\\\\\s*$'` — `\\\\\\\\s*$` terminal. For CRLF line `- **Target:** jrnb2024/pim\\\\\\\\r\\\\\\\\n`: `\\\\\\\\r` is whitespace but not in `[ \\\\\\\\t]`, regex fails. R11-SAFE-MIN-001 carry-forward; unmodified in fix-rounds 1-11.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"All three enforcement paths that parse the target break silently on Windows. The error message 'expected exactly one **Target:** match' does not indicate the cause. Not a bypass — CI fails — but breaks enforcement reliability cross-platform and would frustrate operators on CRLF checkouts.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Change the `**Target:**` regex terminal anchor from `[ \\\\\\\\t]*$` to `\\\\\\\\s*$` at line 212, matching the cascade-status pattern. Add a test DISPATCH-NOTE with \\\\\\\\r\\\\\\\\n line endings to confirm the fix.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"R1-SAFE-MIN-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"Adopter-slug collision is runtime-undetected: `org/repo-foo` and `org/repo.foo` both canonicalize to `org-repo-foo`. An existing STATUS.md TF-024X-renovate-org-repo-foo row from adopter A satisfies the `onboarded-operator-bump` regex check for adopter B (different repo, same slug) without B's operator-bump TF being explicitly filed. D-044 acknowledges as a precondition only.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"check-invocation-log-entry.sh canonicalize_adopter_slug lines 113-116: `re.sub(r'[^a-z0-9-]+', '-', s.replace('/', '-'))` maps both `.` and `_` to `-`. Python pattern line 296: `rf'TF-024X-renovate-{re.escape(slug)}'` — re.search matches any occurrence. D-044: 'Canonical adopter slugs are normalization aids only, not collision-resistant IDs, so cohort repo names must remain unique after canonicalization.' No runtime enforcement. R11-SAFE-MIN-003 carry-forward.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"If a future cohort expansion adds an adopter whose slug collides with an existing one, a stale TF row from the first adopter silently satisfies CI for the second. Threshold A credit could be claimed without the required tracking entry. Named cohort {pim, recommender, shopify-app, mapp-doc-agent, control-tower} has no current collision, but no guard prevents future regressions.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add a collision-detection check at CLI startup: compute canonicalize_adopter_slug for all slugs present in STATUS.md TF-024X-renovate rows and assert the current adopter's slug is unique. Or document the pre-slice collision check as a mandatory operator step in ADOPT-001 §12 per-slice checklist.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"R1-SAFE-NIT-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"Pre-`readlink` `..` pattern check at scaffold-downstream.sh lines 128-132 fires on the RAW path before canonicalization, with exit 1. This redundantly rejects safe paths containing `..` segments (e.g., `~/projects/../pim-clone` resolving to a safe directory), while the actual security denylist at lines 143-147 correctly runs post-canonicalization and is the true security boundary.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"scaffold-downstream.sh lines 128-132: `case \\\\\\\"$OUTPUT_DIR\\\\\\\" in ..|../*|*/../*|*/..) die ...`. Lines 134-137: `readlink -f` / `os.path.realpath` normalization. Lines 143-147: system-path denylist on canonical path. Early check rejects `$REPO_ROOT/../sibling` even if it resolves to a safe non-system path. R11-SAFE-NIT-001 carry-forward.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"No security impact — denylist subsumes protection. Over-restrictive for legitimate `..`-containing paths that resolve to safe directories. Risk: future maintainers might remove the post-canonicalization denylist (lines 143-147) assuming the early check is sufficient, which would be incorrect.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add a comment at line 128 clarifying: 'early UX guard only — not the security boundary; see system-path denylist below which runs after readlink canonicalization.' No code change required.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"R1-SAFE-NIT-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"R11-SAFE-MIN-002 (v1.2.0 tag absent: TF-023E-002 warning silently suppressed) is now downgraded to nit. Current scaffold-downstream.sh lines 155-158 emit an explicit skip-warning when the tag is absent: 'v1.2.0 tag not present in local clone; TF-023E-002 startup_failure check skipped — verify manually if SHA is post-v1.2.0'. The operator is informed the check was skipped. Minor residual: the skip-warning cites a memory project reference which won't resolve in non-SCP environments.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"scaffold-downstream.sh lines 154-159: `if ! git -C \\\\\\\"$SCP_REPO_ROOT\\\\\\\" rev-parse v1.2.0 >/dev/null 2>&1; then warn \\\\\\\"v1.2.0 tag not present in local clone; TF-023E-002 startup_failure check skipped — verify manually if SHA is post-v1.2.0 (memory project_wp_scp_023_state.md). Continuing emission.\\\\\\\"`. test_scaffold_downstream.sh line 218 asserts `grep -Fq 'v1.2.0 tag not present in local clone' \\\\\\\"$missing_cutover_stderr\\\\\\\"`. Fix is present and tested.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Negligible. The operator receives a warning to manually verify. The `(memory project_wp_scp_023_state.md)` reference is SCP-operator-internal and harmless in external use.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Optionally rephrase the skip-warning to omit the memory-system reference: replace `(memory project_wp_scp_023_state.md)` with `(see docs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md §5.3 TF-023E-002)` for external operator clarity. No functional change required.\\\"}]}\\n```\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"1e9bb45f-c3fc-47fb-a64c-2c4c72677eb9\",\"total_cost_usd\":1.9649422499999998,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":16,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":147337,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":1008925,\"output_tokens\":73849,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":147337,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":1,\"output_tokens\":8054,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":150255,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":4463,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":4463},\"type\":\"message\"}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"modelUsage\":{\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\":{\"inputTokens\":1838,\"outputTokens\":26,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":0,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":0,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.001968,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000},\"claude-sonnet-4-6\":{\"inputTokens\":16,\"outputTokens\":73849,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":1008925,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":147337,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":1.9629742499999998,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000}},\"permission_denials\":[],\"terminal_reason\":\"completed\",\"fast_mode_state\":\"off\",\"uuid\":\"56a9a22d-2bad-4b91-a381-fa7d70e35d2a\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/r1-correctness.json\"","aggregated_output":"{\"type\":\"result\",\"subtype\":\"success\",\"is_error\":false,\"api_error_status\":null,\"duration_ms\":1063425,\"duration_api_ms\":1064236,\"num_turns\":14,\"result\":\"I now have everything needed for the review. Producing the JSON output.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"review_id\\\": \\\"R1-CORR-024B-core-2026-05-10\\\",\\n  \\\"slice\\\": \\\"WP-SCP-024/024B-core\\\",\\n  \\\"lens\\\": \\\"correctness\\\",\\n  \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"CHANGES_REQUIRED\\\",\\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"024B-core delivers correct state-machine logic for all 5 cascade-status check paths, correct scaffold-downstream.sh argument validation, a well-formed adopter-wrapper template, and sound adopter-slug canonicalization. No runtime bypass is possible and no CRIT defects are present. Two MAJ test gaps were found: (1) a `return 0` in the pyyaml-absent guard of test_scaffold_downstream.sh silently exits the entire main() function, causing ~200 lines of subsequent test cases (arg validation, denylist, bootstrap guard, master/develop variants) to be skipped on systems without pyyaml — a real exposure since pyyaml is optional per the comment; (2) test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh has no case for `cascade-status: not applicable` + `slice-type: tooling` + `--allow-not-applicable` without `--tooling-slice-id`, leaving the TOOLING_SLICE_ID-required error path untested. Three MIN findings: TF-023E-002 warning fires for the exact v1.2.0 SHA (D-044 says 'post-v1.2.0' but git considers every commit an ancestor of itself); cascade-status absent exits 1 while unrecognised exits 2, inconsistent for the same 'fail-closed default' category; parse_entry_repo() has no test that verifies the em-dash-absent malformed-header path produces a clear failure message. One nit on template vs self-dogfood divergence. Both MAJ items are test fixes only — the underlying enforcement code is correct.\\\",\\n  \\\"findings\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"MAJ-CORR-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MAJ\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"In test_scaffold_downstream.sh, the pyyaml-absent guard at line 133-134 uses `return 0` which exits the entire main() function, not just the YAML lint block. All subsequent test cases (~200 lines: master/develop variants, scorecard-emit=true, non-HEAD SHA warn, bootstrap guard, denylist, arg validation) are silently skipped and the suite exits 0.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"test_scaffold_downstream.sh line 134: `python3 -c 'import yaml' 2>/dev/null || { echo 'SKIP: pyyaml not installed; YAML validation skipped'; return 0; }`. `return 0` inside main() causes main() to exit immediately. Lines 146-413 (non-HEAD SHA, master, develop, CI guard, denylist, invalid-adopter-repo, invalid-branch, short SHA, non-existent SHA, invalid scorecard-emit) are never reached when pyyaml is absent.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"On any CI runner or local environment without pyyaml installed, the scaffolder test suite falsely reports green while skipping coverage of every non-happy-path case including arg validation failures, denylist enforcement, and bootstrap guard — the exact cases that guard against operator error.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Replace `return 0` with a no-op or restructure: `if python3 -c 'import yaml' 2>/dev/null; then python3 - \\\\\\\"$outdir/...\\\\\\\" <<'PY'...; fi`. The YAML lint step should be skipped while main() continues to the remaining test cases.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"MAJ-CORR-002\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MAJ\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh has no test case for `cascade-status: not applicable` + `slice-type: tooling` in DISPATCH-NOTE + `--allow-not-applicable` flag WITHOUT `--tooling-slice-id` flag. The code path (check-invocation-log-entry.sh lines 211-213) correctly dies exit 2 with 'requires --tooling-slice-id', but this is untested.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"check-invocation-log-entry.sh lines 211-213: `case \\\\\\\"$TOOLING_SLICE_ID\\\\\\\" in 024A|024B-core|024B-extras|024G) ;; *) die \\\\\\\"ERROR: --allow-not-applicable requires --tooling-slice-id\\\\\\\" 2 ;; esac`. Empty TOOLING_SLICE_ID matches `*` → die exit 2. Test cases 15b/15c-dup/15d/15f cover adjacent paths but none has slice-type:tooling + --allow-not-applicable + missing --tooling-slice-id simultaneously.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Future refactoring of the TOOLING_SLICE_ID validation block (e.g., reordering the checks) could silently break the missing-tooling-slice-id error path. Given this is a security-sensitive gate (tooling-slice carve-out), the untested path represents a meaningful regression surface.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add test case: DISPATCH-NOTE has `cascade-status: not applicable` + `slice-type: tooling`, call with `--allow-not-applicable` and NO `--tooling-slice-id`. Assert exit 2 with message containing 'requires --tooling-slice-id'.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"MIN-CORR-003\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"scaffold-downstream.sh TF-023E-002 warning fires for the exact v1.2.0 commit SHA, not just descendants. `git merge-base --is-ancestor v1.2.0 $SCP_SHA` returns 0 when SCP_SHA equals the v1.2.0 commit (every commit is its own ancestor in git). D-044 says 'post-v1.2.0 SHAs' but the check includes v1.2.0 itself.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"scaffold-downstream.sh lines 157-158: `elif git -C \\\\\\\"$SCP_REPO_ROOT\\\\\\\" merge-base --is-ancestor v1.2.0 \\\\\\\"$SCP_SHA\\\\\\\" 2>/dev/null; then warn \\\\\\\"--scp-sha $SCP_SHA is post-v1.2.0...\\\\\\\"`. `git merge-base --is-ancestor A A` exits 0 for any commit. D-044: 'emits a TF-023E-002 warning for post-v1.2.0 SHAs'. v1.2.0 is not post-v1.2.0.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"An operator who explicitly passes the v1.2.0 SHA (which does have the attest-scorecard permission ceiling issue) gets the correct warning but is misled by 'post-v1.2.0' wording. Low operational risk since the warning recommends v1.0.0 regardless and v1.2.0 does have the issue.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Change condition to exclude exactly v1.2.0: compare `V1_2_0_SHA=$(git rev-parse v1.2.0)` and use `[ \\\\\\\"$SCP_SHA\\\\\\\" != \\\\\\\"$V1_2_0_SHA\\\\\\\" ] && git merge-base --is-ancestor v1.2.0 \\\\\\\"$SCP_SHA\\\\\\\"`, OR update D-044 wording to 'v1.2.0 or later'.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"MIN-CORR-004\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"cascade-status absent (0 matches from Python `sys.exit(1)`) exits with code 1 via `if ! cascade_status=\\\\\\\"$(…)\\\\\\\"; then exit 1; fi`, while cascade-status unrecognised exits 2 via `die \\\\\\\"FAIL-CLOSED…\\\\\\\" 2`. Both are the 'fail-closed default' category per DISPATCH-NOTE invariant 2 but produce different exit codes for the same conceptual failure.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"check-invocation-log-entry.sh lines 163-179: Python calls `sys.exit(1)` for 0-match case; shell propagates exit 1. Lines 218-220: `die \\\\\\\"FAIL-CLOSED: cascade-status field absent or unrecognised…\\\\\\\" 2` → exit 2 for unrecognised value. DISPATCH-NOTE: 'fail-closed default for absent/unrecognised'. Tests verify exit 1 for absent (case 12) and exit 2 for unrecognised (case 13).\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"024B-extras CI workflow wiring will need to handle both exit codes or treat all non-zero as fail-closed. Complicates downstream error classification if the workflow differentiates 'parsing error' from 'policy rejection'. No security impact — both are non-zero.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Unify by changing the Python `sys.exit(1)` for the absent cascade-status case to `sys.exit(2)` and the shell `exit 1` guard to `exit 2`. Or document the intentional exit-code taxonomy (1=parse-error, 2=policy-rejection) in the script header and test comments.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"MIN-CORR-005\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"parse_entry_repo() in check-invocation-log-entry.sh silently produces 0 matches when a log header uses ASCII hyphens (`---`) instead of the required em-dash (U+2014). The resulting error 'expected exactly one log entry target match, found 0' is opaque — it doesn't tell the operator that the character encoding is wrong.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"parse_entry_repo() at check-invocation-log-entry.sh lines 293-306: regex `[^—]+—\\\\\\\\s+` embeds a literal U+2014 em-dash. Any log header using ASCII `---` would produce zero regex matches → `len(matched) != 1` → `sys.exit(1)` with count error. Comment says 'keep this in sync with the branch-protection log emitter in 024B-extras' but no test validates the malformed-header error message.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add a test case with an ASCII-hyphen header to confirm the error message. Optionally add a secondary check inside parse_entry_repo() that detects `+### ` lines that don't match and emits 'found log header but em-dash separator missing — log format incorrect' before the generic count error.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"nit-CORR-006\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"nit\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"adopter-wrapper.yml.tmpl always emits an explicit `with: scorecard-emit: {{SCORECARD_EMIT}}` block. The SCP self-dogfood wrapper (.github/workflows/policy-check-wrapper.yml) omits the `with:` block entirely (relies on the reusable workflow's default). The DISPATCH-NOTE says the template is a 'mirror of canonical SCP-self wrapper' but the two diverge at this point.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"templates/adopter-wrapper.yml.tmpl lines 19-20: `with:\\\\\\\\n  scorecard-emit: {{SCORECARD_EMIT}}`. .github/workflows/policy-check-wrapper.yml line 76: `uses: …@41a529908ef5355b82ca924ef0502fa5ec2fcc11` with no `with:` block. Functionally equivalent since default is false, but not a literal mirror.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Either accept the divergence and update the DISPATCH-NOTE wording from 'mirror' to 'derived from' the canonical wrapper shape, or note that the explicit scorecard-emit is intentional for adopter clarity.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"892189b3-53ee-4485-a1e4-5a4076199dad\",\"total_cost_usd\":1.60943255,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":9,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":152983,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":386591,\"output_tokens\":61148,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":152983,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":1,\"output_tokens\":3439,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":102777,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":57587,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":57587},\"type\":\"message\"}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"modelUsage\":{\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\":{\"inputTokens\":2397,\"outputTokens\":25,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":0,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":0,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.002522,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000},\"claude-sonnet-4-6\":{\"inputTokens\":9,\"outputTokens\":61148,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":386591,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":152983,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":1.60691055,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000}},\"permission_denials\":[],\"terminal_reason\":\"completed\",\"fast_mode_state\":\"off\",\"uuid\":\"8ee4ecc5-1f05-4748-8b5a-f47570d3829c\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
